## One thread, two queues

JavaScript runs your code on a **single thread**: one statement at a time, to completion. Asynchronous work (timers, network, disk) is handed to the host, and when it finishes, a callback is queued. The **event loop** runs queued callbacks only when the current synchronous code has finished — and it drains **microtasks** (promise reactions, `queueMicrotask`) before it runs the next **macrotask** (`setTimeout`, I/O).

### Worked example

```js
console.log('a');
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('promise'));
console.log('b');
// prints: a, b, promise, timeout
```

Line by line: `'a'` prints immediately. `setTimeout(..., 0)` does NOT run now — it queues a macrotask for "as soon as possible, but after everything else". The `.then` callback queues a **microtask**. `'b'` prints — synchronous code always finishes first. The stack is now empty, so the loop drains microtasks: `'promise'`. Only then does the macrotask queue get a turn: `'timeout'`.

The ordering rule to memorize: **sync code → all microtasks → one macrotask → all microtasks → ...**

An `async` function runs **synchronously until its first `await`**, then suspends; the remainder resumes as a microtask.

### Common mistakes

- Believing `setTimeout(fn, 0)` runs immediately — it always waits for the stack and the microtask queue.
- Blocking the thread with a long loop: NOTHING asynchronous (clicks, timers, promises) can run until it ends.

## Why promises exist

Before promises, async APIs took **error-first callbacks**: `loadUser(id, (err, user) => ...)`. Nesting several of these produces the "pyramid of doom", and every level must remember to check `err`. A **promise** is an object representing a future result. It is in one of three states: **pending**, then settled exactly once as **fulfilled** (with a value) or **rejected** (with a reason, conventionally an `Error`).

### Worked example: wrapping a callback API

```js
function loadUser(id, cb) {               // old style
  setTimeout(() => {
    if (id > 0) cb(null, { id });
    else cb(new Error('bad id'));
  }, 10);
}

function loadUserAsync(id) {              // promise wrapper
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    loadUser(id, (err, user) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else resolve(user);
    });
  });
}
```

Line by line: `new Promise(executor)` runs the executor **immediately**, handing it two functions. We call the legacy API and translate its callback: error → `reject`, success → `resolve`. The promise settles when one of them is called — and only the **first** call counts; later resolve/reject calls are silently ignored. If the executor **throws**, the promise rejects with the thrown error automatically.

This translation is called **promisification**, and it is mechanical enough to automate — you will write the generic version below.

### Common mistakes

- Forgetting to `reject` on the error path — the promise then hangs pending forever.
- Calling `resolve` AND continuing into code that also calls `reject` — the second call is ignored, which can hide bugs.
- Rejecting with strings instead of `Error` objects (you lose the stack trace).

## Chains, rewritten as straight-line code

`async/await` is syntax over promises — no new machinery, just a readable spelling:

```js
async function getUserName(id) {
  const user = await fetchUser(id);   // suspend until the promise settles
  return user.name.toUpperCase();     // fulfills the returned promise
}
```

Line by line: the `async` keyword makes the function **always return a promise**. `await` takes a promise, suspends the function until it settles, and gives you the fulfilled **value** (not the promise). If the awaited promise **rejects**, `await` **throws** that error — catchable with ordinary `try/catch`:

```js
async function safeLoad(id) {
  try {
    return await fetchUser(id);
  } catch (err) {
    return { id, name: 'guest' };    // fallback on failure
  }
}
```

A `throw` inside an async function rejects the returned promise — `throw` and rejection are the same channel, viewed from inside and outside. Awaiting a non-promise value just wraps it (`await 5` is 5 after a microtask). And the rule from the event-loop lesson still holds: the body runs synchronously until the first `await`.

### Common mistakes

- **Forgetting await**: `const n = getNum();` gives you a promise object; `n === 42` is false, `n + 1` is garbage.
- Awaiting sequentially when the operations are independent (the next lesson's whole topic).
- try/catch only catches what you **await** inside it.

## Start together, or one after another?

Awaiting in a loop runs operations **one after another** — total time is the SUM of the delays. When the operations are independent, start them all first and wait once:

```js
// sequential: each await blocks the next start
for (const id of ids) {
  results.push(await fetchItem(id));
}

// parallel: all started before any await
const results = await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchItem(id)));
```

Line by line: in the parallel version, `ids.map(...)` calls `fetchItem` for EVERY id immediately — the work is in flight before anything is awaited. `Promise.all(promises)` returns one promise that fulfills with an **array of results in input order** (not completion order!) once every input fulfills. If ANY input rejects, `Promise.all` rejects immediately with that first error — fail-fast.

Three facts to internalize:

1. `Promise.all` preserves **input order**: `results[i]` matches `promises[i]`, even if `promises[3]` finished first.
2. Non-promise values in the array are allowed (treated as already fulfilled).
3. `Promise.all([])` fulfills immediately with `[]`.

Choose **sequential** when each step needs the previous step's result, or when a resource must not be hit concurrently. Choose **parallel** when the operations are independent — it is the single biggest async performance win.

### Common mistakes

- `items.map(async (x) => ...)` produces an **array of promises** — forgetting the `Promise.all` around it is the classic bug.
- Using parallel calls against something stateful that requires ordering.

## One error channel, several tools

A rejected promise is an error **in transit**: it does nothing until something observes it. Your tools:

- `try { await p } catch (err)` — the awaited rejection re-throws as a catchable exception.
- `p.catch(fn)` — handle on the chain.
- `Promise.allSettled(promises)` — **never rejects**; fulfills with one `{ status, value | reason }` record per input.

### Worked example

```js
async function loadAll(ids) {
  const outcomes = await Promise.allSettled(ids.map(fetchItem));
  const values = [];
  const errors = [];
  for (const o of outcomes) {
    if (o.status === 'fulfilled') values.push(o.value);
    else errors.push(o.reason.message);
  }
  return { values, errors };
}
```

Line by line: every fetch runs in parallel; failures do NOT abort the rest (contrast with `Promise.all`'s fail-fast). Each outcome is a plain object: `{ status: 'fulfilled', value }` or `{ status: 'rejected', reason }`. The loop sorts them into usable buckets — partial success as a first-class result.

**The trap to burn in**: `try/catch` only sees rejections you **await inside the block**. Creating a promise inside `try` and awaiting it later (or never) lets the rejection escape. An unobserved rejection becomes an **unhandled rejection**, which crashes modern Node processes.

### Common mistakes

- `try { const p = fetchX(); } catch {}` — catches nothing; the rejection happens after the block.
- Catching too early and returning `undefined` instead of re-throwing or returning a marked fallback — errors vanish silently.
- Using `Promise.all` when partial success matters.

## The toolkit every codebase rebuilds

Four utilities appear, in some form, in every serious JavaScript project. All of them are small compositions of what you already know.

### delay — the promisified timer

```js
const delay = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
await delay(100);   // pause this async function 100ms
```

`setTimeout` promisified with nothing to pass — resolve fires with `undefined` after the wait. Every other timing utility builds on it.

### timeout — race the work against a deadline

```js
function withTimeout(promise, ms) {
  const deadline = delay(ms).then(() => {
    throw new Error('timed out');
  });
  return Promise.race([promise, deadline]);
}
```

If the work settles first, you get its result; if the deadline fires first, the race rejects. (The losing side keeps running — race never cancels.)

### retry — try again on failure

```js
async function retry(fn, attempts) {
  let lastError;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();           // call fn FRESH each attempt
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
```

The parameter is a **function**, not a promise — a settled promise can never un-settle, so retrying requires manufacturing a NEW promise per attempt. This is the single most common retry bug.

### Common mistakes

- `retry(fetchUser(1), 3)` — passes one doomed promise instead of a factory.
- A timeout wrapper that resolves instead of rejects, making failures look like data.

